<%# The two ways to reach a person, as two separate links.

    There is only one of these in the codebase because there used to be
    thirteen variations of "Call or text <tel: link>", and every one of them
    was a small lie: a tel: href starts a telephone call and offers no way to
    send a message. On a phone that matters -- plenty of customers will text
    a photo of a garage who would not ring a stranger.

    Renders the number as the call target and a separate "text us" as the SMS
    target, so each link does exactly what its words say. Both are built from
    the same BUSINESS_PHONE, so they cannot drift apart.

    Deliberately has no leading verb and no trailing punctuation: the page
    around it supplies "Call ", "or call ", "Rather talk? Call " and so on,
    and that is the only part that differs between the places it is used.

    Renders nothing at all when no phone number is configured. Every call
    site is already inside an `if (businessPhone)`, but a partial that
    silently emits a dead `tel:` link if one ever is not would be exactly the
    kind of thing nobody notices. %>
<% if (businessPhone) { %><a class="text-link" href="tel:<%= telHref(businessPhone) %>"><%= businessPhone %></a> or <a class="text-link" href="sms:<%= smsHref(businessPhone) %>">text us</a><% } %>
